{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>For</b> — Rabbi Moses said: כי means 'because.' The sense is: the wicked man praises because he has attained the desire of his soul, and the greedy man who blesses HaShem—He spurns him. And 'blessed' (ויברך) is like ברך נבות אלהים ומלך and ברך אלהים ומות. The meaning of 'bless' is a gift and offering, and it is a noun, like והדבר אין בהם. And like it: וברך ולא אשיבנה. But this is not correct, for ברך is a past-tense verb, as if written 'he blessed a blessing and I will not return it.' And in my opinion, 'for the wicked praises' refers to another wicked man, mentioned above, who desired and blessed the greedy man who spurned HaShem. And here 'blessed' (ויברך) is like 'praises.'"], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes Rabbi Moses's reading of כי as 'because' (כי תחת אשר), then refutes it, then offers his own reading where the subject of 'praises' shifts to a different wicked man than the one mentioned earlier in the psalm."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:26:52.285964019Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}